window.docContents[52].resolve({"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Euclidean-balls-and-spheres":{"contents":"For a center , a scalar function , and any\n, defineOn a positive-radius sphere , this is the derivative in\nthe outward radial unit direction. The definition remains total at \nbecause Mathlib defines normalize 0 = 0.\n\nThe definition is written as a coordinate sum so that it matches the\ncomponentwise form of the ball Gauss theorem. Arguments that prefer the\nintrinsic gradient use the inner-product form.\n\nThe coordinate definition agrees everywhere with the Euclidean gradient\npairing:No differentiability hypothesis is needed for this equality because both\nsides use the same totalized Fréchet derivative.\n\nExpand the Euclidean-space inner product as the finite sum of coordinate\ninner products. Over , each scalar inner product is ordinary\nmultiplication. Each gradient coordinate is the corresponding partial derivative.\n\nThe boundary term of the divergence theorem on a ball is the pairing of the\nfield with the radial unit vector , the same\nvector that appears in the normal derivative. Unlike the box case, a sphere is\nnot a union of coordinate faces, so the theorem is not a corollary of\nMathlib's box divergence theorem and is proved by a radial differentiation\nargument instead.\n\nAssume , , and let every component\n be globally . ThenEquivalently, on the sphere where ,\n\nWriteFirst prove, for positive  near , the compactly supported weighted\nidentityThis is the integration-by-parts step; the positive-part quadratic cutoff\nremoves any boundary-at-infinity term.Differentiate this identity once with respect to . The derivative of the\nleft side is , while the radial differentiation formula\nfor the right side gives . On\n, writeSubstitute this identity and cancel the nonzero factor . This yields the\nnormal-flux formula.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.4. Euclidean balls and spheres","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Euclidean-balls-and-spheres"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Weak-solutions":{"contents":"Momentum test fields are smooth and compactly supported and vanish at the\nterminal time, but they may be nonzero at .  Integration by parts then\ngivesThe integrability predicate requires spatial integrability of the combined\nmomentum integrand at every physical time, and time integrability of its\nspatial integral rather than of each summand. The initial pairing is\nintegrable by the  initial datum and compactly supported smooth test.\nThis pressure-retaining identity is used\nby a weak solution with a pressure representative; solenoidal tests remove its pressure term.\n\nA pressure-free weak solution tests momentum only against solenoidal fields. It stores a velocity with  slices at every time in\n, an explicit distributional spatial gradient  with \nslices and weak-derivative identities at every time in , the combined\niterated integrability of the momentum integrand, weak incompressibility of\nevery velocity slice, and the initial datum as a boundary functional. The\ninitial datum is required only to lie in , and forcing has \nspatial slices at every physical time. The viscous term uses , never a\nclassical derivative of the weak velocity.A weak solution with a pressure representative additionally supplies an  pressure slice at\nevery physical time and the pressure-retaining identity for all momentum\ntests. Restricting to solenoidal tests gives its checked projection to\na pressure-free weak solution; the converse would require pressure reconstruction.\n\nThe componentwise gradient witnesses use the\nweak-derivative relation developed in the\nSobolev chapter. The fluid formulation adds the momentum identity,\nincompressibility, pressure, and time dependence; it keeps the same weak\ncalculus.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.5. Weak solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Weak-solutions"}});